import java.util.Scanner;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        int base = sc.nextInt();
        int exp = sc.nextInt();
        int n = sc.nextInt();

        System.out.println("power(" + base + ", " + exp + ") = " + power(base, exp));
        System.out.println("digitSum(" + n + ") = " + digitSum(n));
    }

    static long power(int base, int exp) {
        if (exp == 0) {
            return 1;
        }
        return base * power(base, exp - 1);
    }

    static int digitSum(int n) {
        if (n < 10) {
            return n;
        }
        return n % 10 + digitSum(n / 10);
    }
}

steps.body:
To recurse over an array or a String, pass an index that says where you are. The base case is the index reaching the end, and each call moves it by one. This is cheaper than `s.substring(1)`, which copies the rest of the String on every call.

Callers should not have to supply that index. Write a `public` entry method that starts the recursion, and a `private` overload with the same name that carries the index:

```java
public static int sum(int[] a) {
    return sum(a, 0);                // entry point: start at index 0
}

private static int sum(int[] a, int i) {
    if (i == a.length) {
        return 0;                    // past the end: nothing left
    }
    return a[i] + sum(a, i + 1);
}
```

Overloading lets both share one name because their parameter lists differ. Strings work the same way with `s.length()` and `s.charAt(i)`. A helper can also stop early: return `false` the moment a check fails, and the waiting frames pass that answer straight back up.

Be careful with the base case test: use `i == a.length`. Stopping at `i == a.length - 1` either skips the last element or, on an empty array, reads past the end.

