import java.util.Scanner;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        String word = sc.next();
        char target = sc.next().charAt(0);

        System.out.println("count = " + count(word, target));
        System.out.println("palindrome = " + isPalindrome(word));
    }

    public static int count(String s, char c) {
        return count(s, c, 0);
    }

    public static boolean isPalindrome(String s) {
        return isPalindrome(s, 0);
    }

    private static int count(String s, char c, int i) {
        if (i == s.length()) {
            return 0;
        }
        int here = s.charAt(i) == c ? 1 : 0;
        return here + count(s, c, i + 1);
    }

    private static boolean isPalindrome(String s, int i) {
        if (i >= s.length() / 2) {
            return true;
        }
        if (s.charAt(i) != s.charAt(s.length() - 1 - i)) {
            return false;
        }
        return isPalindrome(s, i + 1);
    }
}

steps.body:
Some recursions call themselves more than once. Fibonacci numbers add the two before them: `fib(n) = fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)`. Written directly, every call branches into two, and the same values are computed over and over. `fib(30)` makes more than two million calls, and `fib(50)` makes tens of billions. The work grows exponentially.

**Memoization** fixes it: save each answer the first time you compute it, and return the saved copy after that. For small non-negative `int` inputs, a `long[] memo` indexed by `n` is the simplest store:

```java
private static long fib(int n, long[] memo) {
    if (n < 2) {
        return n;
    }
    if (memo[n] != 0) {
        return memo[n];              // computed before
    }
    memo[n] = fib(n - 1, memo) + fib(n - 2, memo);
    return memo[n];
}
```

Now each `n` is computed once, so `fib(90)` needs fewer than 200 calls. Using 0 to mean "not computed yet" works because every saved answer is positive. If 0 can be a real answer, `Arrays.fill(memo, -1)` first. When keys are sparse or not small ints, a `Map<Integer, Long> cache = new HashMap<>()` does the same job with `containsKey`, `get`, and `put`.

Answers still grow: `fib(92)` is the largest Fibonacci number that fits in a `long`.

