steps.body:
Divide and conquer splits a problem and recurses only on the part that matters. Binary search is the classic case. In a **sorted** array, look at the middle element. If it is the target, you are done. If the target is bigger, it can only be in the right half, otherwise only in the left half. Each call throws away half the range, so a million elements need about 20 calls and the stack stays shallow.

Track the range with `lo` and `hi`, both inclusive. An empty range, `lo > hi`, is the base case and means the target is missing:

```java harness
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        int[] a = {1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11};
        System.out.println("7 -> " + search(a, 7, 0, a.length - 1));
        System.out.println("4 -> " + search(a, 4, 0, a.length - 1));
    }

    {{code}}
}
```

```java
private static int search(int[] a, int target, int lo, int hi) {
    if (lo > hi) {
        return -1;
    }
    int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
    if (a[mid] == target) {
        return mid;
    }
    // recurse on mid + 1..hi or lo..mid - 1
    if (a[mid] < target) {
        return search(a, target, mid + 1, hi);
    }
    return search(a, target, lo, mid - 1);
}
```

Write `lo + (hi - lo) / 2`, not `(lo + hi) / 2`. On a huge array `lo + hi` can pass `Integer.MAX_VALUE` and wrap to a negative index.

The array must be sorted. On unsorted data binary search does not crash, it just gives wrong answers. With duplicates, it returns whichever match it lands on first, which is not always the first one.

